--TEST--
An `array<E, T>` takes only cases of `E` as keys, has no append, and `array<K, T>` needs an enum `K` (`rule:types/arrays`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/an-enum-as-a-type
// Each refused line below is one mistake and gets its own error.
// A number or a string becomes a key only after `as Priority` checks it.
enum Priority { Low, Medium, High }
enum Size { Small, Large }

array<Priority, string> $labels = [Priority::Low => "low"];

// Refused: an `int` is not a case.
$labels[1] = "medium";

// Refused: a `string` is not a case.
$labels["2"] = "high";

// Refused: a case of another enum.
$labels[Size::Small] = "small";

// Refused: an enum-keyed array has no next key to append under.
$labels[] = "other";

// Refused: only an enum can be the key type.
array<string, int> $counts = [];

// Accepted: `as Priority` checks the number first.
$labels[1 as Priority] = "medium";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0434]: the keys of this array are cases of `Priority`, and this key is `int`
   --> case.nvs:11:9
%A
error[E0434]: the keys of this array are cases of `Priority`, and this key is `string`
   --> case.nvs:14:9
%A
error[E0434]: the keys of this array are cases of `Priority`, and this key is `Size`
   --> case.nvs:17:9
%A
error[E1018]: `[]` cannot add a value to an array whose keys are enum cases
   --> case.nvs:20:1
%A
error[E1017]: `string` is not an enum, so it cannot be the key type of an array
   --> case.nvs:23:7
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
